Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether every syzygy $(P_1,\ldots,P_n)$ of the relation $\sum_a k_a P_a = 0$ can be expressed via an antisymmetric matrix of polynomials $P_{ab}$ such that $P_a = \sum_b k_b P_{ab}$.

The answer is **yes**. Here is a self-contained proof by induction on $n$.

---

**Proof.**

Let $R = \mathbb{R}[k_1,\ldots,k_n]$. We prove by induction on $n$ the following statement:

> (*) If $P_1,\ldots,P_n \in R$ satisfy $\sum_{a=1}^n k_a P_a = 0$, then there exist polynomials $P_{ab} \in R$ ($1 \le a,b \le n$) with $P_{ab} = -P_{ba}$ and $P_a = \sum_{b=1}^n k_b P_{ab}$ for all $a$.

**Base case $n=1$.** The relation $k_1 P_1 = 0$ in the integral domain $R$ forces $P_1 = 0$. Setting $P_{11} = 0$ (the only antisymmetric option) gives $P_1 = k_1 P_{11} = 0$. ✓

**Inductive step.** Assume (*) holds for $n-1$ variables. Let $P_1,\ldots,P_n \in \mathbb{R}[k_1,\ldots,k_n]$ satisfy $\sum_{a=1}^n k_a P_a = 0$.

**Step 1: Reduce modulo $k_n$.** Setting $k_n = 0$ in the relation gives
$$\sum_{a=1}^{n-1} k_a \, P_a\big|_{k_n=0} = 0$$
in $\mathbb{R}[k_1,\ldots,k_{n-1}]$. By the inductive hypothesis, there exist antisymmetric polynomials $\widetilde{P}_{ab} \in \mathbb{R}[k_1,\ldots,k_{n-1}]$ (for $1 \le a,b \le n-1$) such that
$$P_a\big|_{k_n=0} = \sum_{b=1}^{n-1} k_b \, \widetilde{P}_{ab}, \qquad a = 1,\ldots,n-1.$$

**Step 2: Extract the $k_n$-part.** Since $P_a - \sum_{b=1}^{n-1} k_b \widetilde{P}_{ab}$ vanishes when $k_n = 0$, it is divisible by $k_n$. So there exist $Q_a \in R$ with
$$P_a = \sum_{b=1}^{n-1} k_b \, \widetilde{P}_{ab} + k_n \, Q_a, \qquad a = 1,\ldots,n-1.$$

**Step 3: Determine $P_n$.** Substitute into the original relation:
$$\sum_{a=1}^{n-1} k_a \left(\sum_{b=1}^{n-1} k_b \widetilde{P}_{ab} + k_n Q_a\right) + k_n P_n = 0.$$

The double sum vanishes by antisymmetry:
$$\sum_{a,b=1}^{n-1} k_a k_b \widetilde{P}_{ab} = \sum_{1 \le a < b \le n-1} k_a k_b \bigl(\widetilde{P}_{ab} + \widetilde{P}_{ba}\bigr) = 0.$$

So we obtain $k_n\!\left(\sum_{a=1}^{n-1} k_a Q_a + P_n\right) = 0$. Since $R$ is an integral domain and $k_n \ne 0$,
$$P_n = -\sum_{a=1}^{n-1} k_a \, Q_a.$$

**Step 4: Assemble the antisymmetric matrix.** Define:
$$P_{ab} = \begin{cases} \widetilde{P}_{ab} & \text{if } 1 \le a,b \le n-1, \\ Q_a & \text{if } 1 \le a \le n-1,\; b = n, \\ -Q_b & \text{if } a = n,\; 1 \le b \le n-1, \\ 0 & \text{if } a = b = n. \end{cases}$$

*Antisymmetry:* For $a,b < n$ this is inherited from $\widetilde{P}_{ab}$. For the cross terms, $P_{an} = Q_a = -P_{na}$. The diagonal $P_{aa} = 0$ throughout. ✓

*Decomposition for $a < n$:*
$$\sum_{b=1}^n k_b P_{ab} = \sum_{b=1}^{n-1} k_b \widetilde{P}_{ab} + k_n Q_a = P_a. \quad ✓$$

*Decomposition for $a = n$:*
$$\sum_{b=1}^n k_b P_{nb} = \sum_{b=1}^{n-1} k_b (-Q_b) + k_n \cdot 0 = -\sum_{b=1}^{n-1} k_b Q_b = P_n. \quad ✓$$

This completes the induction. $\blacksquare$

**Remark.** This result is the exactness of the Koszul complex at the first stage for the regular sequence $k_1,\ldots,k_n$ in the polynomial ring — a special case of Hilbert's syzygy theorem. The inductive proof above is a direct, self-contained verification.

